{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MRRX7UztNAZwGUMuDkwg8P","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"timezone abbreviations confusing problems developers\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"List of time zone abbreviations - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_time_zone_abbreviations\"},{\"title\":\"List of tz database time zones - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tz_database_time_zones\"},{\"title\":\"Bug #82230 “Time zone abbreviations can cause confusion” : Bugs : Launchpad itself\",\"url\":\"https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/82230\"},{\"title\":\"Time Zone Abbreviations - Worldwide List\",\"url\":\"https://www.timeanddate.com/time/zones/\"},{\"title\":\"Falsehoods programmers believe about time zones\",\"url\":\"https://www.zainrizvi.io/blog/falsehoods-programmers-believe-about-time-zones/\"},{\"title\":\"Timezone abbreviations to name and UTC offset · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/alyssaq/f1f0ec50e79f1c089554d0de855dd09c\"},{\"title\":\"Timezone Names and Abbreviations\",\"url\":\"https://docs.precisely.com/docs/sftw/precisely-apis/main/en-us/webhelp/apis/Appendix/appendix_e_timezones.html\"},{\"title\":\"List of Time Zone Abbreviations\",\"url\":\"https://www.utctime.net/time-zone-abbreviations\"},{\"title\":\"Appendix A: Time zone abbreviations | Online Manual\",\"url\":\"https://www.advtimesync.com/online-manual/appendix-a-time-zone-abbreviations\"},{\"title\":\"Time Zones for Software Developers | by Bikash Paneru | Better Programming\",\"url\":\"https://betterprogramming.pub/time-zones-for-software-developers-7f21d5a407aa\"}]\n\nHere are the search results about timezone abbreviations and the problems they cause for developers:\n\n## Key Problems with Timezone Abbreviations\n\n### Ambiguity of Abbreviations\n\"CST\" can mean China Standard Time (UTC+08:00), Cuba Standard Time (UTC−05:00), and (North American) Central Standard Time (UTC−06:00), and it is also a widely used variant of ACST (Australian Central Standard Time, UTC+9:30).(no content)It's particularly bad due to EST not just meaning US and Australian Eastern Standard Time, but also Australian Eastern Summer Time. This has caused real confusion for developers, as one user noted being confused when times were cited as EST during Australian summer time, and there's no way to check what offset it is or convert it to local time.\n\n### Common Misconceptions\nThe time zone name changes during Daylight Saving Time, but many people don't know that and keep using the wrong abbreviations during DST months. CST might be used for Central Daylight Time.(no content)If there's no standard name for time zones, can you really expect one for the abbreviations? When converting Eastern Standard Time to Pakistan Standard Time, it's unclear if you're talking about American or Australian Eastern Standard Time.\n\n### Database Inconsistencies\nSome zone records use 3 or 4 letter abbreviations that are tied to physical time zones, others use numeric UTC offsets.(no content)The time zone database used by Launchpad is \"pytz\", which in turn uses the Olson time zone data (the same data used by most UNIX systems). That data uses the \"EST\" abbreviation for Australian Eastern Standard/Summer Time.\n\n### Recommended Solutions\nReplacing the abbreviations with offsets, or just using UTC now that most things use the new timestamp display format, would make things easier.(no content)UTC is the world's time standard. This means that the time in every other part of the world is determined using UTC. The local time anywhere in the world can be obtained by adding or subtracting an offset from UTC.\n\n### Additional Complexity\nWhen DST ends and the clock moves backwards (like 1:30 am on Nov 1st, 2020 in the US), 1:30am occurs twice that morning—how do you know which instance was intended?(no content)Amsterdam was once at the UTC + 19 minute, 32.13 second offset.\n\nThe general recommendation for developers is to use the IANA/Olson timezone database identifiers (like \"America/New_York\" or \"Australia/Sydney\") instead of ambiguous abbreviations, and to store timestamps in UTC whenever possible.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"99d7cc46-8cec-4e6d-92ef-e02e008c6f1c","uuid":"bec9b7ae-1a1e-4563-af2d-435bc5ad701e","tool_use_result":{"query":"timezone abbreviations confusing problems developers","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01TmsoefvCucWcYQquHnAwYJ","content":[{"title":"List of time zone abbreviations - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_time_zone_abbreviations"},{"title":"List of tz database time zones - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tz_database_time_zones"},{"title":"Bug #82230 “Time zone abbreviations can cause confusion” : Bugs : Launchpad itself","url":"https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/82230"},{"title":"Time Zone Abbreviations - Worldwide List","url":"https://www.timeanddate.com/time/zones/"},{"title":"Falsehoods programmers believe about time zones","url":"https://www.zainrizvi.io/blog/falsehoods-programmers-believe-about-time-zones/"},{"title":"Timezone abbreviations to name and UTC offset · GitHub","url":"https://gist.github.com/alyssaq/f1f0ec50e79f1c089554d0de855dd09c"},{"title":"Timezone Names and Abbreviations","url":"https://docs.precisely.com/docs/sftw/precisely-apis/main/en-us/webhelp/apis/Appendix/appendix_e_timezones.html"},{"title":"List of Time Zone Abbreviations","url":"https://www.utctime.net/time-zone-abbreviations"},{"title":"Appendix A: Time zone abbreviations | Online Manual","url":"https://www.advtimesync.com/online-manual/appendix-a-time-zone-abbreviations"},{"title":"Time Zones for Software Developers | by Bikash Paneru | Better Programming","url":"https://betterprogramming.pub/time-zones-for-software-developers-7f21d5a407aa"}]},"Here are the search results about timezone abbreviations and the problems they cause for developers:\n\n## Key Problems with Timezone Abbreviations\n\n### Ambiguity of Abbreviations\n\"CST\" can mean China Standard Time (UTC+08:00), Cuba Standard Time (UTC−05:00), and (North American) Central Standard Time (UTC−06:00), and it is also a widely used variant of ACST (Australian Central Standard Time, UTC+9:30).(no content)It's particularly bad due to EST not just meaning US and Australian Eastern Standard Time, but also Australian Eastern Summer Time. This has caused real confusion for developers, as one user noted being confused when times were cited as EST during Australian summer time, and there's no way to check what offset it is or convert it to local time.\n\n### Common Misconceptions\nThe time zone name changes during Daylight Saving Time, but many people don't know that and keep using the wrong abbreviations during DST months. CST might be used for Central Daylight Time.(no content)If there's no standard name for time zones, can you really expect one for the abbreviations? When converting Eastern Standard Time to Pakistan Standard Time, it's unclear if you're talking about American or Australian Eastern Standard Time.\n\n### Database Inconsistencies\nSome zone records use 3 or 4 letter abbreviations that are tied to physical time zones, others use numeric UTC offsets.(no content)The time zone database used by Launchpad is \"pytz\", which in turn uses the Olson time zone data (the same data used by most UNIX systems). That data uses the \"EST\" abbreviation for Australian Eastern Standard/Summer Time.\n\n### Recommended Solutions\nReplacing the abbreviations with offsets, or just using UTC now that most things use the new timestamp display format, would make things easier.(no content)UTC is the world's time standard. This means that the time in every other part of the world is determined using UTC. The local time anywhere in the world can be obtained by adding or subtracting an offset from UTC.\n\n### Additional Complexity\nWhen DST ends and the clock moves backwards (like 1:30 am on Nov 1st, 2020 in the US), 1:30am occurs twice that morning—how do you know which instance was intended?(no content)Amsterdam was once at the UTC + 19 minute, 32.13 second offset.\n\nThe general recommendation for developers is to use the IANA/Olson timezone database identifiers (like \"America/New_York\" or \"Australia/Sydney\") instead of ambiguous abbreviations, and to store timestamps in UTC whenever possible."],"durationSeconds":17.02133299999999}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M5wP9jWDQKwBhjXLQuUNUE","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"IANA time zone database tzdb explained\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Time zone and daylight saving time data\",\"url\":\"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tz-link.html\"},{\"title\":\"tz database - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tz_database\"},{\"title\":\"Time Zone Database\",\"url\":\"https://www.iana.org/time-zones\"},{\"title\":\"How to Read the tz Database\",\"url\":\"https://ftp.iana.org/tz/tzdb-2020e/tz-how-to.html\"},{\"title\":\"IANA (TZDB) time zone information\",\"url\":\"https://nodatime.org/TimeZones\"},{\"title\":\"List of tz database time zones - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tz_database_time_zones\"},{\"title\":\"IANA time zone\",\"url\":\"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2021a/zone1970.tab\"},{\"title\":\"Theory and pragmatics of the tz code and data\",\"url\":\"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2022b/theory.html\"},{\"title\":\"Sources for time zone and daylight saving time data\",\"url\":\"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2019c/tz-link.html\"},{\"title\":\"How to Read the tz Database\",\"url\":\"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2024a/tz-how-to.html\"}]\n\nHere's what I found about the IANA Time Zone Database (tzdb):\n\n## Overview\n\nThe tz database is a collaborative compilation of information about the world's time zones and rules for observing daylight saving time, primarily intended for use with computer programs and operating systems.(no content)Paul Eggert has been its editor and maintainer since 2005.(no content)The Time Zone Database (often called tz or zoneinfo) contains code and data that represent the history of local time for many representative locations around the globe. It is updated periodically to reflect changes made by political bodies to time zone boundaries, UTC offsets, and daylight-saving rules.\n\n## Key Features\n\nThis database (known as tz, tzdb, or zoneinfo) is used by several implementations, including the GNU C Library (used in GNU/Linux), Android, FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD, ChromiumOS, Cygwin, MariaDB, MINIX, musl libc, MySQL, webOS, AIX, iOS, macOS, Microsoft Windows, OpenVMS, Oracle Database, and Oracle Solaris.(no content)Each main entry in the database represents a timezone for a set of civil-time clocks that have all agreed since 1970. Timezones are typically identified by continent or ocean and then by the name of the largest city within the region containing the clocks.\n\n## Structure and Format\n\nThe tz database attempts to record the history and predicted future of civil time scales. It organizes time zone and daylight saving time data by partitioning the world into timezones whose clocks all agree about timestamps that occur after the POSIX Epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC).(no content)For example, applications that deal with current and future timestamps in the traditional North American mountain time zone can choose from the timezones America/Denver which observes US-style daylight saving time (DST), America/Mazatlan which observes Mexican-style DST, and America/Phoenix which does not observe DST.\n\n## Time Zone Naming\n\nCanonical - The primary, preferred zone name. Link - An alternative name (alias) which links to a canonical zone.(no content)The LETTER (sometimes called LETTER/S) column can contain a variable part of the usual abbreviation of the time zone's name, or it can just be a hyphen if there's no variable part. For example, the abbreviation used in the central time zone will be either \"CST\" or \"CDT\". The variable part is 'S' or 'D'.\n\n## Maintenance\n\nIts management procedure is documented in BCP 175: Procedures for Maintaining the Time Zone Database.(no content)Proposed changes are sent to the tz mailing list, which is gatewayed to the comp.time.tz Usenet newsgroup. Source files are distributed via the IANA FTP server.(no content)The IESG-designated TZ Coordinators are Paul Eggert (primary) and Tim Parenti (secondary).\n\n## History\n\nThe project's origins go back to 1986 or earlier.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"99d7cc46-8cec-4e6d-92ef-e02e008c6f1c","uuid":"24ced6eb-f80c-44bd-8422-dd93d82b8667","tool_use_result":{"query":"IANA time zone database tzdb explained","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01F7N9kLVDoh6Gn2UhykSArB","content":[{"title":"Time zone and daylight saving time data","url":"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tz-link.html"},{"title":"tz database - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tz_database"},{"title":"Time Zone Database","url":"https://www.iana.org/time-zones"},{"title":"How to Read the tz Database","url":"https://ftp.iana.org/tz/tzdb-2020e/tz-how-to.html"},{"title":"IANA (TZDB) time zone information","url":"https://nodatime.org/TimeZones"},{"title":"List of tz database time zones - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tz_database_time_zones"},{"title":"IANA time zone","url":"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2021a/zone1970.tab"},{"title":"Theory and pragmatics of the tz code and data","url":"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2022b/theory.html"},{"title":"Sources for time zone and daylight saving time data","url":"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2019c/tz-link.html"},{"title":"How to Read the tz Database","url":"https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2024a/tz-how-to.html"}]},"Here's what I found about the IANA Time Zone Database (tzdb):\n\n## Overview\n\nThe tz database is a collaborative compilation of information about the world's time zones and rules for observing daylight saving time, primarily intended for use with computer programs and operating systems.(no content)Paul Eggert has been its editor and maintainer since 2005.(no content)The Time Zone Database (often called tz or zoneinfo) contains code and data that represent the history of local time for many representative locations around the globe. It is updated periodically to reflect changes made by political bodies to time zone boundaries, UTC offsets, and daylight-saving rules.\n\n## Key Features\n\nThis database (known as tz, tzdb, or zoneinfo) is used by several implementations, including the GNU C Library (used in GNU/Linux), Android, FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD, ChromiumOS, Cygwin, MariaDB, MINIX, musl libc, MySQL, webOS, AIX, iOS, macOS, Microsoft Windows, OpenVMS, Oracle Database, and Oracle Solaris.(no content)Each main entry in the database represents a timezone for a set of civil-time clocks that have all agreed since 1970. Timezones are typically identified by continent or ocean and then by the name of the largest city within the region containing the clocks.\n\n## Structure and Format\n\nThe tz database attempts to record the history and predicted future of civil time scales. It organizes time zone and daylight saving time data by partitioning the world into timezones whose clocks all agree about timestamps that occur after the POSIX Epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC).(no content)For example, applications that deal with current and future timestamps in the traditional North American mountain time zone can choose from the timezones America/Denver which observes US-style daylight saving time (DST), America/Mazatlan which observes Mexican-style DST, and America/Phoenix which does not observe DST.\n\n## Time Zone Naming\n\nCanonical - The primary, preferred zone name. Link - An alternative name (alias) which links to a canonical zone.(no content)The LETTER (sometimes called LETTER/S) column can contain a variable part of the usual abbreviation of the time zone's name, or it can just be a hyphen if there's no variable part. For example, the abbreviation used in the central time zone will be either \"CST\" or \"CDT\". The variable part is 'S' or 'D'.\n\n## Maintenance\n\nIts management procedure is documented in BCP 175: Procedures for Maintaining the Time Zone Database.(no content)Proposed changes are sent to the tz mailing list, which is gatewayed to the comp.time.tz Usenet newsgroup. Source files are distributed via the IANA FTP server.(no content)The IESG-designated TZ Coordinators are Paul Eggert (primary) and Tim Parenti (secondary).\n\n## History\n\nThe project's origins go back to 1986 or earlier."],"durationSeconds":18.64864941700001}}
